Create a 9:16 editorial double-exposure poster where the portrait and secondary imagery actively shape the layout, rather than simply filling a face silhouette with scenery.

Use a large photographic portrait as the visual anchor, keeping the key facial features clear and recognizable. Blend the secondary imagery selectively through the hair, neck, shoulders, or part of the face, with natural transitions and clear visual hierarchy.

Build the composition around one strong layout device such as an arch, oversized type crossing the portrait, architectural sections extending beyond the figure, vertical image slices, or geometric framing. Let typography overlap, hide behind, or cut through the portrait so it becomes part of the composition.

Keep the secondary imagery meaningful to the theme and limit it to a few strong elements instead of random collage. Use clear scale changes, negative space, and controlled overlap to create depth.

Typography should feel editorial and intentional: one strong title, a few small information blocks, and no filler copy. Use restrained paper texture, subtle film grain, and refined print-like contrast.

Avoid generic face-filled-with-landscape compositions, equal-opacity overlays, duplicated facial features, cluttered collage, excessive text, random UI elements, fake credits, numbering, watermarks, or copyright marks.

